## Hono body-parsing dispatch

### `parseBody` — the only Content-Type switch

**`node_modules/hono/dist/utils/body.js:3–10`** (source: `src/utils/body.ts`)

```js
var parseBody = async (request, options = Object.create(null)) => {
  const { all = false, dot = false } = options;
  const headers = request instanceof HonoRequest ? request.raw.headers : request.headers;
  const contentType = headers.get("Content-Type");          // line 6
  if (contentType?.startsWith("multipart/form-data") ||     // line 7
      contentType?.startsWith("application/x-www-form-urlencoded")) {
    return parseFormData(request, { all, dot });             // line 8
  }
  return {};                                                 // line 10 — everything else → empty object
};
```

This is the **only** place Hono inspects `Content-Type` to route body parsing. It handles exactly two content-type prefixes:

| Content-Type prefix | Parser called | Mechanism |
|---|---|---|
| `multipart/form-data` | `parseFormData` → `request.formData()` | Web Fetch API |
| `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` | `parseFormData` → `request.formData()` | Web Fetch API |
| anything else | returns `{}` | nothing |

`parseBody` is called lazily and memoized at **`request.js:56`**:
```js
async parseBody(options) {
  return this.bodyCache.parsedBody ??= await parseBody(this, options);
}
```

---

### JSON and text have no auto-dispatch

There is **no Content-Type check for `application/json` or `text/*`** in `parseBody`. JSON and text are entirely separate, explicit methods backed by `cachedBody` at **`request.js:58–78`**:

```js
cachedBody = (key) => {
  const { bodyCache, raw } = this;
  const cachedBody = bodyCache[key];
  if (cachedBody) return cachedBody;                        // line 62 — memoize
  const anyCachedKey = Object.keys(bodyCache)[0];
  if (anyCachedKey) {
    return bodyCache[anyCachedKey].then((body) => {         // line 66 — re-derive from already-consumed body
      if (anyCachedKey === "json") body = JSON.stringify(body);
      return new Response(body)[key]();
    });
  }
  return bodyCache[key] = raw[key]();                       // line 73 — delegate to Web Fetch API
};

json()        { return this.cachedBody("json"); }           // line 76 — req.json()  → raw.json()
text()        { return this.cachedBody("text"); }           // line 79 — req.text()  → raw.text()
arrayBuffer() { return this.cachedBody("arrayBuffer"); }
blob()        { return this.cachedBody("blob"); }
formData()    { return this.cachedBody("formData"); }
```

These delegate straight to the [Web Fetch API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Request) methods on the underlying `raw` request. The `cachedBody` wrapper ensures the body stream is only consumed once even if multiple methods are called — if a body was already read under one key, it re-derives from the cached result.

---

### Summary

- **`req.parseBody()`** — only auto-routes `multipart/form-data` and `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` (`utils/body.js:7`). Everything else returns `{}`.
- **`req.json()`** — no Content-Type check at all; you call it explicitly; delegates to `raw.json()` (`request.js:76`).
- **`req.text()`** — same, explicit only (`request.js:79`).
- The **memoization cache** (`bodyCache`) prevents double-consuming the stream for any of these paths (`request.js:58–73`).
